Turkish has eight vowels, but they are not those eight. The eight in Turkis=
h=20
are a, e, =C4=B1, i, o, =C3=B6, u, =C3=BC. AFAIK Turkish and some related l=
anguages are the=20
only ones with this three-bit vowel system: three phonetic features can be=
=20
true or false, all eight possibilities are used, and they are in alphabetic=
al=20
order. Finnish and German also have eight vowels, but they have a slightly=
=20
different set: no =C4=B1, but =C3=A4. (a, o, u), (e, i), (=C3=A4, =C3=B6, =
=C3=BC/y). "=C4=B1" is like=20
Russian yery (lips unrounded like i, tongue back like u); "y" in Finnish is=
=20
the same as "=C3=BC" in German (lips rounded like u, tongue forward like i)=
.

